<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanjak of Smederevo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1459">1459</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Pashalik of Belgrade</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a>, later known as the Pashalik of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, was an Ottoman province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> created after the conquest of Smederevo in 1459 and governed from Belgrade after 1521. In 1801 renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>, the dahije, seized power there, and their terror provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> of 1804. Its territory became the core of the autonomous Principality of Serbia from 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanjak-of-smederevo.jpg" alt="Sanjak of Smederevo" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgrade_pashaluk.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> was formed after the fall of the Serbian Despotate in 1459, with its administrative seat at Smederevo, then defended by the imposing Smederevo Fortress. It belonged to Rumelia Eyalet at first, later to Budin Eyalet from 1541 to 1686, and for periods to Temeșvar Eyalet. In the 1470s, fighting with the Hungarians left many areas of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> deserted, and Ottoman sources note a migration of Vlach pastoralists into the sanjak, who were settled as a military element. In 1476 there were 7,600 Vlach households and 15,000 peasant households. Tax registrations of 1476 and 1516 show that about 17 per cent of villages had been abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Ottoman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1521 the seat moved to that city, though the name stayed the same. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces took Belgrade in 1688 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> recaptured it in 1690. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> occupied the sanjak again in 1717 and received it in 1718 by the Treaty of Požarevac, holding it for twenty years as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> until the Ottomans retook it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a>. Austrian forces held Belgrade once more from 1788 to 1791.</p><p>In 1793 and 1796 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a> issued firmans that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> more rights, including tax collection by the obor-knez, and freedom of trade and religion. Hadji Mustafa Pasha governed from 1793 to 1801 and was seen as a trusted ally of the sultan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> whom Selim III wished to remove found refuge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-pazvantoglu">Osman Pazvantoğlu</a>, a rebel in the Sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, whose raids caused fear in the region. By 1799 the janissaries had returned under a pardon and suspended Serbian autonomy, raised taxes and imposed martial law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 15 December 1801 Hadji Mustafa Pasha was murdered by Kučuk Alija, one of the four leading dahije, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> commanders who opposed the sultan's reforms. The sanjak was then ruled by these renegade janissaries independently of the Ottoman government. On 4 February 1804 several district chiefs were killed in the Slaughter of the Knezes, which sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> (1804–13), the first phase of the Serbian Revolution. The uprising was suppressed in 1813, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a>, led by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>, produced the semi-independent Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Serbia gained full independence in 1878 and became a kingdom in 1882.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanjak of Smederevo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He backed the janissary dahije who seized power in the <b>Pashalik of Belgrade</b>, whose abuses helped provoke the Serbian uprising of 1804.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-vidin">Pashalik of Vidin</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1801 renegade janissaries, the dahije, seized power in the <b>Pashalik of Belgrade</b> and terrorised the Serbian population.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
